1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
In August 1858, Queen Victoria sent a message to the president

2
00:00:04,036 --> 00:00:05,382
of the United States.

3
00:00:05,455 --> 00:00:08,073
It took about sixteen hours to get through.

4
00:00:08,145 --> 00:00:09,891
That was worth celebrating.

5
00:00:10,036 --> 00:00:13,600
Until then, news had crossed the Atlantic at the speed of a ship.

6
00:00:13,673 --> 00:00:17,309
This message had traveled through a wire lying on the ocean floor.

7
00:00:17,455 --> 00:00:19,782
Within weeks, the wire went silent.

8
00:00:19,855 --> 00:00:22,255
It still stretched from Ireland to Newfoundland.

9
00:00:22,327 --> 00:00:24,582
Crews had risked their ships getting it there.

10
00:00:24,764 --> 00:00:28,364
Now an operator could send a signal into the cable and no one on the other

11
00:00:28,473 --> 00:00:29,575
shore could read it.

12
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:31,782
To make the connection work again, they'd have to solve

13
00:00:31,855 --> 00:00:33,745
two very different problems.

14
00:00:33,927 --> 00:00:36,473
One was how to lay a cable across an ocean.

15
00:00:36,509 --> 00:00:38,764
The other was how to make a whisper of electricity

16
00:00:38,836 --> 00:00:40,545
come out of it as a message.

17
00:00:40,582 --> 00:00:44,000
Cyrus Field, an American businessman, had spent years persuading

18
00:00:44,182 --> 00:00:46,436
investors and governments to back the link.

19
00:00:46,473 --> 00:00:48,291
The idea was easy to picture.

20
00:00:48,364 --> 00:00:51,491
Stretch the telegraph wire that already carried messages across land

21
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:53,345
all the way across the Atlantic.

22
00:00:53,382 --> 00:00:55,782
In practice, the wire needed a waterproof coat

23
00:00:55,855 --> 00:00:58,727
around the copper and a jacket of iron to protect it.

24
00:00:58,764 --> 00:01:01,236
The finished cable was too heavy for any one of the ships

25
00:01:01,309 --> 00:01:02,618
available to carry it.

26
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,073
So a British warship, the Agamemnon, took one half.

27
00:01:06,109 --> 00:01:08,909
An American ship, the Niagara, took the other.

28
00:01:08,945 --> 00:01:13,600
On the first expedition, in 1857, the cable snapped after hundreds of miles

29
00:01:13,673 --> 00:01:15,382
had already gone over the side.

30
00:01:15,418 --> 00:01:17,564
There wasn't enough left to start again.

31
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,109
The following year they tried a different maneuver.

32
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:21,855
Meet in the middle of the ocean.

33
00:01:21,891 --> 00:01:23,164
Join the two ends.

34
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,400
Then sail away from each other, with one ship headed for Ireland

35
00:01:26,473 --> 00:01:28,036
and the other for Newfoundland.

36
00:01:28,073 --> 00:01:30,836
The cable would sink behind them while the operators on the ships

37
00:01:30,909 --> 00:01:32,557
used it to stay in touch.

38
00:01:32,606 --> 00:01:34,400
A storm battered the fleet.

39
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:36,909
Then another break forced them to turn back.

40
00:01:36,945 --> 00:01:40,545
On the next voyage the two ships finally reached opposite shores.

41
00:01:40,582 --> 00:01:44,266
For the first time, a telegraph message could cross the Atlantic.

42
00:01:44,315 --> 00:01:47,055
The operators could get a message through, but they had to work

43
00:01:47,236 --> 00:01:49,891
painfully slowly. A telegraph sends a message

44
00:01:49,964 --> 00:01:52,036
as a series of electrical pulses.

45
00:01:52,073 --> 00:01:55,673
If the pulses reach the other end distinctly, an operator can read them.

46
00:01:55,709 --> 00:01:59,200
Over the length of this cable they arrived weak and spread out.

47
00:01:59,273 --> 00:02:02,764
Send the pulses too close together and they start to run into each other.

48
00:02:02,836 --> 00:02:05,564
Wildman Whitehouse, the company's chief electrician,

49
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:08,000
drove the cable with high voltages to make its weak

50
00:02:08,218 --> 00:02:09,527
signals easier to detect.

51
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:12,509
But pushing the cable harder put more stress on the very

52
00:02:12,582 --> 00:02:16,582
thing that kept it working underwater: the insulation around the copper.

53
00:02:16,655 --> 00:02:18,400
The signals deteriorated.

54
00:02:18,473 --> 00:02:20,727
The line that had carried Victoria's telegram stopped

55
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:22,473
working within a month.

56
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:24,655
There was another way to read those signals.

57
00:02:24,727 --> 00:02:27,855
William Thomson, a Scottish physicist on the project, had built

58
00:02:27,891 --> 00:02:30,036
a receiver around a tiny mirror.

59
00:02:30,109 --> 00:02:33,309
A weak electric current could turn the mirror by a fraction.

60
00:02:33,382 --> 00:02:36,800
The mirror threw a spot of light across a scale, making the movement large

61
00:02:36,836 --> 00:02:38,618
enough for the operator to see.

62
00:02:38,691 --> 00:02:41,382
With a more sensitive receiver, a faint pulse could carry

63
00:02:41,673 --> 00:02:44,145
information without the sender having to force such a strong

64
00:02:44,218 --> 00:02:45,455
one into the wire.

65
00:02:45,527 --> 00:02:48,218
The mirror addressed the problem of hearing the signal.

66
00:02:48,291 --> 00:02:51,891
The next expedition still needed a cable that could survive the sea.

67
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:54,291
Field secured backing for another attempt.

68
00:02:54,327 --> 00:02:57,915
This time the cable had better insulation and stronger protection.

69
00:02:57,939 --> 00:03:02,364
And by 1865 there was finally a ship that could take the whole length.

70
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,055
The Great Eastern had been built to carry passengers.

71
00:03:05,091 --> 00:03:08,461
Its cavernous hull now held immense coils of cable.

72
00:03:08,485 --> 00:03:11,164
The crew could watch the signal as they paid out the line.

73
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:13,673
If they detected a fault, they could stop and haul part

74
00:03:13,709 --> 00:03:15,600
of the cable back up to fix it.

75
00:03:15,636 --> 00:03:18,206
That mattered when defects appeared along the way.

76
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:21,782
The men found the damaged sections, repaired them and carried on.

77
00:03:21,818 --> 00:03:25,382
Then, with Newfoundland still about six hundred miles away, they stopped

78
00:03:25,418 --> 00:03:26,727
for another repair.

79
00:03:26,764 --> 00:03:29,564
As they were bringing cable back up, it snapped.

80
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,745
The free end dropped to the seabed.

81
00:03:31,782 --> 00:03:33,927
The crew lowered hooks to try to catch it.

82
00:03:33,964 --> 00:03:36,727
They couldn't get it back aboard, and the Great Eastern sailed

83
00:03:36,873 --> 00:03:38,812
home without finishing the line.

84
00:03:38,861 --> 00:03:42,182
In July 1866, the Great Eastern set out from Ireland

85
00:03:42,327 --> 00:03:44,545
again with another improved cable.

86
00:03:44,582 --> 00:03:48,582
This time it reached Heart's Content, Newfoundland, with the line intact.

87
00:03:48,655 --> 00:03:50,982
Operators on either shore could pass messages back

88
00:03:51,055 --> 00:03:53,891
and forth over a connection that stayed in service.

89
00:03:53,964 --> 00:03:55,879
Then the ship went back out.

90
00:03:55,903 --> 00:03:59,055
There was still a nearly complete cable lying on the bottom, the one

91
00:03:59,127 --> 00:04:00,764
they'd lost the year before.

92
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:03,127
If they could lift the end, they could join a new length

93
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:04,994
to it and finish the job.

94
00:04:05,043 --> 00:04:07,830
The crew finally caught it and raised it to the deck.

95
00:04:07,879 --> 00:04:10,145
The test came in the ship's electrical room.

96
00:04:10,182 --> 00:04:13,309
With the old line hauled up from the seabed, they waited to see whether

97
00:04:13,382 --> 00:04:15,018
Ireland could still reach them.

98
00:04:15,055 --> 00:04:18,000
On Thomson's instrument, the spot of light moved.

99
00:04:18,036 --> 00:04:19,855
The cable was still alive.

100
00:04:19,927 --> 00:04:23,273
They joined it to fresh cable and brought that line ashore too.

101
00:04:23,382 --> 00:04:26,982
Eight years earlier, one cable had lasted only a few weeks.

102
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,109
Now the Atlantic had two working telegraph lines.

103
00:04:30,182 --> 00:04:32,800
A ship still took days to reach the other shore.

104
00:04:32,909 --> 00:04:34,473
Its news could already be there.

